! ============================================================
! 02b  Integration-grid convergence  (run all three)
! ============================================================
! DFT, unlike HF, evaluates part of the energy by NUMERICAL
! QUADRATURE on a grid of points around the nuclei.  The grid
! is an approximation with its own error, independent of the
! basis set, and it is the one beginners forget.
!
! Run grids 1, 3 and 7 and collect TWO numbers from each:
!
!    grid   points   Integrated N_e      E (Eh)
!      1      8250   9.99993792        -76.464125
!      3     64398   10.00000097       -76.464212
!      7     61578   10.00000002       -76.464213
!
! Note that grid 7 has FEWER points than grid 3 here and is
! still the more accurate of the two: what matters is how the
! points are placed, not how many there are.  Grid 3 uses the
! Mura-Knowles radial map, grid 7 the Euler-Maclaurin one with
! a different pruning scheme.
!
! HOW TO READ IT.  "Integrated N_e" is the number of electrons
! recovered by integrating the density on the grid.  The exact
! answer is known in advance -- here 10 -- so this one number
! measures the quadrature error with no reference calculation
! and no ambiguity.  Make a habit of glancing at it.
!
! A deviation of 1e-3 electrons or worse means the grid is too
! coarse for what you are doing, and energy DIFFERENCES will
! be noisy even if the total energy looks fine.  Grid error is
! also the reason a DFT geometry optimization can stall or a
! frequency can come out slightly imaginary: the quadrature
! makes the surface faintly bumpy.
!
! Rule of thumb: grid 3 (the default) for exploration, grid 6
! or better for frequencies and for anything involving weak
! interactions.
%method  BP86
%basis   6-311++G(2d,2p)
%job     energy
%grid    7
%charge  0
%mult    1

%geo
 O   0.0000000   0.0000000   0.1173000
 H   0.0000000   0.7572000  -0.4692000
 H   0.0000000  -0.7572000  -0.4692000
